 o:idmap v:ext="edit" data="1" />\n</o:shapelayout></xml><![endif]-->\n</head
 >\n<body lang="EN-US" link="#0563C1" vlink="#954F72">\n<div class="WordSecti
 on1">\n<p class="MsoNormal">Dear all\,<o:p></o:p></p>\n<p class="MsoNormal">
 <o:p>&nbsp\;</o:p></p>\n<p class="MsoNormal">Here&#8217\;s the invitation\, 
 please use the link below.<o:p></o:p></p>\n<p class="MsoNormal"><o:p>&nbsp\;
 </o:p></p>\n<p class="MsoNormal">Cheers\, Henrik<o:p></o:p></p>\n<p class="M
 soNormal"><o:p>&nbsp\;</o:p></p>\n<div>\n<p class="MsoNormal">-----Original 
 Appointment-----<br>\n<b>From:</b> Barber\, Thomas &lt\;thomas.barber@sap.co
 m&gt\; <br>\n<b>Sent:</b> Wednesday\, May 6\, 2020 11:47 AM<br>\n<b>To:</b> 
 Barber\, Thomas\; DL Security_Research_Exchange<br>\n<b>Cc:</b> &#8230\;<br>
 \n<b>Subject:</b> [Research Exchange] Thou Shalt Not Depend on Me and When C
 oding Style Survives Compilation<br>\n<b>When:</b> Friday\, May 15\, 2020 11
 :00 AM-12:00 PM (UTC&#43\;01:00) Amsterdam\, Berlin\, Bern\, Rome\, Stockhol
 m\, Vienna.<br>\n<b>Where:</b> Microsoft Teams Meeting<o:p></o:p></p>\n</div
 >\n<p class="MsoNormal"><o:p>&nbsp\;</o:p></p>\n<p class="MsoNormal">Dear Re
 search Exchangers\,<o:p></o:p></p>\n<p class="MsoNormal"><o:p>&nbsp\;</o:p><
 /p>\n<p class="MsoNormal">As you may have heard in the last Research Exchang
 e session\, I will be taking the reins as organizer of the Research Exchange
  sessions from Peter. Thanks again Peter for a great job over the last year!
 <o:p></o:p></p>\n<p class="MsoNormal"><o:p>&nbsp\;</o:p></p>\n<p class="MsoN
 ormal">This Research Exchange will be presented by a candidate for an open P
 hD position in the field of Open Source Security. The candidate will present
  the following two papers:<o:p></o:p></p>\n<p class="MsoNormal"><o:p>&nbsp\;
 </o:p></p>\n<ul style="margin-top:0in" type="disc">\n<li class="MsoPlainText
 " style="mso-list:l1 level1 lfo3"><a href="https://seclab.ccs.neu.edu/static
 /publications/ndss2017jslibver.pdf">&#8221\;Thou Shalt Not Depend on Me: Ana
 lysing the Use of Outdated JavaScript Libraries on the Web&#8221\; by Lauing
 er et al. (2017)</a><o:p></o:p></li></ul>\n<p class="MsoPlainText"><o:p>&nbs
 p\;</o:p></p>\n<p class="MsoPlainText" style="margin-left:.25in">Abstract&#8
 212\;Web developers routinely rely on third-party JavaScript libraries such 
 as jQuery to enhance the functionality of their sites. However\, if not prop
 erly maintained\, such dependencies can create attack\n vectors allowing a s
 ite to be compromised. In this paper\, we conduct the first comprehensive st
 udy of client-side JavaScript library usage and the resulting security impli
 cations across the Web. Using data from over 133 k websites\, we show that 3
 7 % of them\n include at least one library with a known vulnerability\; the 
 time lag behind the newest release of a library is measured in the order of 
 years. In order to better understand why websites use so many vulnerable or 
 outdated libraries\, we track causal inclusion\n relationships and quantify 
 different scenarios. We observe sites including libraries in ad hoc and ofte
 n transitive ways\, which can lead to different versions of the same library
  being loaded into the same document at the same time. Furthermore\, we find
  that\n libraries included transitively\, or via ad and tracking code\, are 
 more likely to be vulnerable. This demonstrates that not only website admini
 strators\, but also the dynamic architecture and developers of third-party s
 ervices are to blame for the Web&#8217\;s poor\n state of library management
 . The results of our work underline the need for more thorough approaches to
  dependency management\, code maintenance and third-party code inclusion on 
 the Web.<o:p></o:p></p>\n<p class="MsoPlainText"><o:p>&nbsp\;</o:p></p>\n<ul
  style="margin-top:0in" type="disc">\n<li class="MsoPlainText" style="mso-li
 st:l1 level1 lfo3"><a href="https://www.sec.cs.tu-bs.de/pubs/2018-ndss.pdf">
 &#8221\;When Coding Style Survives Compilation: De-anonymizing Programmers f
 rom Executable Binaries&#8221\; by Islam et al. (2018)</a><o:p></o:p></li></
 ul>\n<p class="MsoPlainText"><o:p>&nbsp\;</o:p></p>\n<p class="MsoPlainText"
  style="margin-left:.25in">Abstract&#8212\;The ability to identify authors o
 f computer programs based on their coding style is a direct threat to the pr
 ivacy and anonymity of programmers. While recent work found that source code
  can be attributed\n to authors with high accuracy\, attribution of executab
 le binaries appears to be much more difficult. Many distinguishing features 
 present in source code\, e.g. variable names\, are removed in the compilatio
 n process\, and compiler optimization may alter the structure\n of a program
 \, further obscuring features that are known to be useful in determining aut
 horship. We examine programmer de-anonymization from the standpoint of machi
 ne learning\, using a novel set of features that include ones obtained by de
 compiling the executable\n binary to source code. We adapt a powerful set of
  techniques from the domain of source code authorship attribution along with
  stylistic representations embedded in assembly\, resulting in successful de
 anonymization of a large set of programmers. We evaluate\n our approach on d
 ata from the Google Code Jam\, obtaining attribution accuracy of up to 96% w
 ith 100 and 83% with 600 candidate programmers. We present an executable bin
 ary authorship attribution approach\, for the first time\, that is robust to
  basic obfuscations\,\n a range of compiler optimization settings\, and bina
 ries that have been stripped of their symbol tables. We perform programmer d
 e-anonymization using both obfuscated binaries\, and real-world code found &
 #8220\;in the wild&#8221\; in single-author GitHub repositories and\n the re
 cently leaked Nulled.IO hacker forum. We show that programmers who would lik
 e to remain anonymous need to take extreme countermeasures to protect their 
 privacy.<o:p></o:p></p>\n<p class="MsoNormal"><o:p>&nbsp\;</o:p></p>\n<p cla
 ss="MsoNormal">As always\, you can find recordings of the past sessions here
 :<o:p></o:p></p>\n<p class="MsoNormal"><a href="https://jam4.sapjam.com/grou
 ps/KxkEs5HqefZnmgxTYUSSov/overview_page/o8jKcdR9SqgKFWvhNK0koM">https://jam4
 .sapjam.com/groups/KxkEs5HqefZnmgxTYUSSov/overview_page/o8jKcdR9SqgKFWvhNK0k
 oM</a><o:p></o:p></p>\n<p class="MsoNormal">And change your subscription to 
 the Security_Research_Exchange distribution list here:<o:p></o:p></p>\n<p cl
 ass="MsoNormal"><a href="https://profiles.wdf.sap.corp/groups/5cb5d886b4a586
 027f8b069e">https://profiles.wdf.sap.corp/groups/5cb5d886b4a586027f8b069e</a
 ><o:p></o:p></p>\n<p class="MsoNormal"><o:p>&nbsp\;</o:p></p>\n<p class="Mso
 Normal">Best regards\,<o:p></o:p></p>\n<p class="MsoNormal"><o:p>&nbsp\;</o:
 p></p>\n<p class="MsoNormal"><o:p>&nbsp\;</o:p></p>\n<p class="MsoNormal"><o
 :p>&nbsp\;</o:p></p>\n<p class="MsoNormal">Tom<o:p></o:p></p>\n<p class="Mso
 Normal"><o:p>&nbsp\;</o:p></p>\n<p class="MsoNormal"><span style="color:gray
 ">__________________________________________________________________________
