BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:18abface-9ad3-4cf5-8820-561dad804608
SUMMARY:SLIMFAST kickoff meeting
DESCRIPTION:Hi\, \n\nWe plan to perform a kickoff meeting about the SLIMFAST
  project -- the project Xhevahire is involved in! \nIt's about configuration
 s options\, software variability\, compilers\, performance\, security\, and 
 machine learning (a tentative abstract below) \n\nThere will be talks of Xhe
 vahire\, Luc\, Hugo\, and me. \nMore details will follow. \nWe will record t
 he sessions and be live. \n\nFeel free to join and *discuss* \;) \n\nabstrac
 t: After 10+ years of research in configurable systems\, I can safely admit 
 that configuration can be more a problem than a solution: who can and wants 
 to tune hundreds of options' values? I can also unsafely add that configurat
 ion is a security threat: configuration options may be an unnecessary opport
 unity and entry point for attackers. \nThe idea of SLIMFAST is conceptually 
 simple: as some configuration options can be pointless and represent a threa
 t\, we should automatically remove them from the actual code and system. The
  missing piece behind this vision is that there is no automatic way to "spec
 ialize" a running system. There are two key challenges: (1) identifying irre
 levant options' values (e.g.\, because options are more keen to vulnerabilit
 ies or because the execution time is too slow)\; (2) removing the options-re
 lated code. We aim to develop a "specializer" that would take as input a set
  of preset options' values and produces a new source code that can then be f
 urther exploited by compilers (e.g.\, through dead code elimination). The be
 t is that removing configuration options can have ripple\, positive effects 
 on many non-functional properties of a software system. Being slim/debloated
 \, the software could well run faster\, consume less energy\, and be more se
 cured -- for some of the remaining configurations. \n
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<html><body id='htmlmode'><h3>Nouvelle demande 
 de réunion ci-dessous :</h3>\n\n<p>\n<table border='0'>\n<tr><th align=left>
 Sujet :</th><td>SLIMFAST kickoff meeting </td></tr>\n<tr><th align=left>Orga
 nisateur:</th><td>"Mathieu Acher" &lt\;mathieu.acher@irisa.fr&gt\; </td></tr
 >\n</table>\n<p>\n<table border='0'>\n<tr><th align=left>Endroit :</th><td>"
 Rennes-Salle Lipari (F202 Vidéoprojecteur\, Visioconférence)" &lt\;rennes-sa
 lle_lipari@zimbra-local.inria.fr&gt\; </td></tr>\n<tr><th align=left>Ressour
 ces :</th><td>"Rennes-Salle Lipari (F202 Vidéoprojecteur\, Visioconférence)"
  &lt\;rennes-salle_lipari@zimbra-local.inria.fr&gt\; ((Capacité salle divisé
 e par 2 si crise sanitaire) - Lieu : Rennes\, Bâtiment : F\, Étage : orange\
 , Salle : F202) </td></tr>\n<tr><th align=left>Heure:</th><td>Jeudi 15 Octob
 re 2020\, 14:00:00 - 17:00:00 GMT +01:00 Bruxelles\, Copenhague\, Madrid\, P
 aris\n </td></tr></table>\n<p>\n<table border='0'>\n<tr><th align=left>Invit
 és:</th><td>diverse@inria.fr\; t.xheva@gmail.com\; Philippe.Collet@univ-cote
 dazur.fr\; gurvan.le_guernic@inria.fr </td></tr>\n</table>\n<div>*~*~*~*~*~*
 ~*~*~*~*</div><br><div style="font-family: arial\, helvetica\, sans-serif\; 
 font-size: 12pt\; color: #000000"><div>Hi\,&nbsp\;</div><div><br data-mce-bo
 gus="1"></div><div>We plan to perform a kickoff meeting about the SLIMFAST p
 roject -- the project Xhevahire is involved in!&nbsp\;<br data-mce-bogus="1"
 ></div><div>It's about configurations options\, software variability\, compi
 lers\, performance\, security\, and machine learning (a tentative abstract b
 elow)</div><div><br data-mce-bogus="1"></div><div>There will be talks of Xhe
 vahire\, Luc\, Hugo\, and me.&nbsp\;</div><div>More details will follow.&nbs
 p\;</div><div>We will record the sessions and be live.&nbsp\;</div><div><br 
 data-mce-bogus="1"></div><div>Feel free to join and *discuss* \;)&nbsp\;</di
 v><div><br data-mce-bogus="1"></div><div>abstract:&nbsp\;<span style="font-s
 ize: 12pt\;">After 10+ years of research in configurable systems\, I can saf
 ely admit that configuration can be more a problem than a solution: who can 
 and wants to tune hundreds of options' values? I can also unsafely add that 
 configuration is a security threat: configuration options may be an unnecess
 ary opportunity and entry point for attackers.&nbsp\;</span></div><div><div>
 The idea of SLIMFAST is conceptually simple: as some configuration options c
 an be pointless and represent a threat\, we should automatically remove them
  from the actual code and system. The missing piece behind this vision is th
 at there is no automatic way to "specialize" a running system. There are two
  key challenges: (1) identifying irrelevant options' values (e.g.\, because 
 options are more keen to vulnerabilities or because the execution time is to
 o slow)\; (2) removing the options-related code.&nbsp\;We aim to develop a "
 specializer" that would take as input a set of preset options' values and pr
 oduces a new source code that can then be further exploited by compilers (e.
 g.\, through dead code elimination). The bet is that removing configuration 
 options can have ripple\, positive effects on many non-functional properties
  of a software system. Being slim/debloated\, the software could well run fa
 ster\, consume less energy\, and be more secured -- for some of the remainin
 g configurations.&nbsp\;</div></div></div></body></html>
LOCATION:"Rennes-Salle Lipari (F202 Vidéoprojecteur\, Visioconférence)" <ren
 nes-salle_lipari@zimbra-local.inria.fr>
