BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20100909T114529Z-8e3cd-2ee0-a780012-Oracle
SUMMARY:[O]séminaire Ascola : Centering a Language Design around Resources (
 Eric Tanter)
DESCRIPTION:Abstract:\nAs programming moves beyond algorithms and towards co
 mponent composition\nand interaction with the world\, managing software reso
 urces becomes\nincreasingly difficult.  Resources are objects whose use is c
 onstrained\nin some way.  Example constraints include initializing resources
  before\nuse\, cleaning up resources when they are no longer needed\, or fol
 lowing\nprotocols such as checking for permission before performing an actio
 n on\na resource.\n\nThis talk describes how the Plaid programming language\
 , currently being designed and implemented at CMU\, is designed to facilitat
 e programming with resources.  Rather than being an instance of a fixed clas
 s\, every object in Plaid is in a state that is changeable. Like a class\, e
 ach state has its own interface\, representation\, and behavior\, allowing t
 he source code to naturally model the conceptual evolution of objects.\n\nIn
  order to help the programmer reason about resources\, Plaid's\npermission-b
 ased type system tracks the state of objects and their\naliases.  Types are 
 optional\, and cast-like permission assertions enable\nthe dynamic recovery 
 of aliasing knowledge.  Aliasing information in the\ntype system allows Plai
 d programs to execute concurrently whenever\noperations are provably noninte
 rfering.  Together\, these features\nsupport cleaner modeling of resource co
 nstraints\, effective support in\nchecking them\, and efficient parallel exe
 cution on emerging multicore\nprocessors.
LOCATION:salle visio E220 - Evert
