BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:228c8ec5-1fde-4411-90c2-b70e402f2258
SUMMARY:[SEMINAR] Modeling Variability From Requirements to Runtime (M. Ache
 r)
DESCRIPTION:\n\nComplex software systems exhibit high degrees of variability
  \nalong several dimensions. From a technical and software perspective\, \nt
 he number of components\, their variations due to choices among \npossible a
 lgorithms\, the different ways to assemble them\, the number \nof tunable pa
 rameters\, etc. make the system configuration rather \nchallenging. Moreover
 \, the number of different applications that the \nsystem covers\, the envir
 onments and contexts where they run\, the \nquality of service that they req
 uire increase the difficulty. Finally \nthe context of an application may (a
 nd does) change in real time\, at \nruntime\, requiring dynamic reconfigurat
 ion of the system. \nTo make things even more complex\, these variability fa
 ctors are not \nindependent: they are related by a tangled set of strong con
 straints. \nThis huge variability raises problems at design time (finding th
 e \nconfigurations needed by the system\, foreseeing the different possible 
 \ncontexts)\, at deployment time (selecting the initial configuration)\, \na
 nd at run time (switching configurations to react to context \nchanges). \nO
 ur own experience in the development of video surveillance processing \nchai
 ns has pointed out similar issues. \nIn this talk\, I will present a modelin
 g process in which variability \nsources are separated in different feature 
 models (a widely used \nformalism in software product line engineering). \nT
 he feature models are used to describe the context of execution and \nthe re
 quirements while others are an abstract representation of the \ncode. \nThe 
 feature models are inter-related by propositional constraints \nwhile consis
 tency checking and propagation of variability choices are \nautomated. \nIn 
 particular\, we guarantee the reachability property\, i.e.\, that for \nall 
 valid specifications and contexts\, there exists at least one valid \nsoftwa
 re configuration. \nWe show how the variability requirements can be expresse
 d and then \nrefined at design time so that the set of valid software \nconf
 igurations to be considered at runtime may be highly reduced. \nSoftware too
 ls and the FAMILIAR language support the approach and some \nexperimentation
 s on a video surveillance product line are reported. \nFinally\, I will pres
 ent our recent effort towards fully integrating \nthe models in a run time a
 daptation architecture so that an \nend-to-end engineering of the video surv
 eillance product line can be \nmade possible. \n
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<html><body><h3>La réunion suivante a été modif
 iée :</h3>\n\n<p>\n<table border='0'>\n<tr><th align=left>Sujet :</th><td>[S
 EMINAR] Modeling Variability From Requirements to Runtime (M. Acher) </td></
 tr>\n<tr><th align=left>Organisateur:</th><td>"Benoit Combemale" &lt\;benoit
 .combemale@irisa.fr&gt\; </td></tr>\n</table>\n<p>\n<table border='0'>\n<tr>
 <th align=left>Heure:</th><td>Jeudi 9 Février 2012\, 14:30:00 - 15:30:00 GMT
  +01:00 Bruxelles\, Copenhague\, Madrid\, Paris [MODIFIÉ]\n </td></tr></tabl
 e>\n<p>\n<table border='0'>\n<tr><th align=left>Invités:</th><td>"An Phung-K
 hac" <an.phungkhac@inria.fr>\; "Julien Richard-Foy" <julien.richard-foy@iris
 a.fr>\; "Joao Ferreira_filho" <joao.ferreira_filho@inria.fr>\; "Suresh Pilla
 y" <suresh.pillay@irisa.fr>\; "Jean-Emile Dartois" <jean-emile.dartois@inria
 .fr>\; "Jonathan Marchand" <jonathan.yves.marchand@irisa.fr>\; "Hanen Haouas
 " <hanen.haouas@inria.fr>\; "Xavier Dolques" <xavier.dolques@inria.fr>\; "Ta
 m Le_nhan" <tam.le_nhan@inria.fr>\; "Aymeric Hervieu" <aymeric.hervieu@irisa
 .fr>\;  ... </td></tr>\n</table>\n<div>*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*</div><br><div sty
 le='font-family:Times New Roman\; font-size: 12pt\; color: #000000\;'><div s
 tyle="font-family:Times New Roman\; font-size: 12pt\; color: #000000\;"><div
  style="font-family:Times New Roman\; font-size: 12pt\; color: #000000\;">Co
 mplex software systems exhibit high degrees of variability<br>along several 
 dimensions. From a technical and software perspective\,<br>the number of com
 ponents\, their variations due to choices among<br>possible algorithms\, the
  different ways to assemble them\, the number<br>of tunable parameters\, etc
 . make the system configuration rather<br>challenging. Moreover\, the number
  of different applications that the<br>system covers\, the environments and 
 contexts where they run\, the<br>quality of service that they require increa
 se the difficulty. Finally<br>the context of an application may (and does) c
 hange in real time\, at<br>runtime\, requiring dynamic reconfiguration of th
 e system.<br>To make things even more complex\, these variability factors ar
 e not<br>independent: they are related by a tangled set of strong constraint
 s.<br>This huge variability raises problems at design time (finding the<br>c
 onfigurations needed by the system\, foreseeing the different possible<br>co
 ntexts)\, at deployment time (selecting the initial configuration)\,<br>and 
 at run time (switching configurations to react to context<br>changes).<br>Ou
 r own experience in the development of video surveillance processing<br>chai
 ns has pointed out similar issues.<br>In this talk\, I will present a modeli
 ng process in which variability<br>sources are separated in different featur
 e models (a widely used<br>formalism in software product line engineering).<
 br>The feature models are used to describe the context of execution and<br>t
 he requirements while others are an abstract representation of the<br>code.<
 br>The feature models are inter-related by propositional constraints<br>whil
 e consistency checking and propagation of variability choices are<br>automat
 ed.<br>In particular\, we guarantee the reachability property\, i.e.\, that 
 for<br>all valid specifications and contexts\, there exists at least one val
 id<br>software configuration.<br>We show how the variability requirements ca
 n be expressed and then<br>refined at design time so that the set of valid s
 oftware<br>configurations to be considered at runtime may be highly reduced.
 <br>Software tools and the FAMILIAR language support the approach and some<b
 r>experimentations on a video surveillance product line are reported.<br>Fin
 ally\, I will present our recent effort towards fully integrating<br>the mod
 els in a run time adaptation architecture so that an<br>end-to-end engineeri
 ng of the video surveillance product line can be<br>made possible.<br></div>
 </div></div></body></html>
