BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:2365adba-3347-40d6-84da-d6e150b77a77
SUMMARY:DiverSE Coffee
DESCRIPTION:Hi all\, \n\nFor our next DiverSE coffee\, please welcome Kevin 
 Corre\, our Carambar riddles passionate! \nKevin is carrying out a joint PhD
  program at DiverSE & Orange Labs. \nHis topics of interest include Identity
  Management in the Web context. \n\nEnters Kevin : \n"I will present the rec
 ent progress on my thesis work (including two articles this year!) and the w
 ork to come. \nI will recall the basis of WebRTC\, and in particular WebRTC 
 security architecture. The objective is to raise the security level of a Web
 RTC communication on demand. \nTo do that\, I focused on the explicit authen
 tication system and explored two directions: to allow users to choose which 
 IdP to use and to negotiate over the other party authentication. Implementat
 ion details included. \;)" \n\nDon't come empty handed! Bring all your unsol
 ved riddles and come enjoy his talk! \n
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<html><body id='htmlmode'><h3>The following is 
 a new meeting request:</h3>\n\n<p>\n<table border='0'>\n<tr><th align=left>S
 ubject:</th><td>DiverSE Coffee </td></tr>\n<tr><th align=left>Organiser:</th
 ><td>"Amine Benelallam" &lt\;amine.benelallam@inria.fr&gt\; </td></tr>\n</ta
 ble>\n<p>\n<table border='0'>\n<tr><th align=left>Location:</th><td>"Rennes-
 Salle Minquiers (B025)" &lt\;rennes-salle_minquiers@zimbra-local.inria.fr&gt
 \; </td></tr>\n<tr><th align=left>Resources:</th><td>"Rennes-Salle Minquiers
  (B025)" &lt\;rennes-salle_minquiers@zimbra-local.inria.fr&gt\; (Lieu : Renn
 es\, Bâtiment : B\, Étage : bleu\, Salle : B025) </td></tr>\n<tr><th align=l
 eft>Time:</th><td>Thursday\, 27 April\, 2017\, 1:00:00 PM - 2:00:00 PM GMT +
 01:00 Brussels\, Copenhagen\, Madrid\, Paris\n </td></tr></table>\n<p>\n<tab
 le border='0'>\n<tr><th align=left>Invitees:</th><td>diverse@inria.fr </td><
 /tr>\n</table>\n<div>*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*</div><br><div style="font-family: t
 imes new roman\, new york\, times\, serif\; font-size: 12pt\; color: #000000
 "><div>Hi all\,&nbsp\;</div><div><br></div><div>For our next DiverSE coffee\
 , please welcome Kevin Corre\, our Carambar&nbsp\;riddles passionate! &nbsp\
 ;</div><div>Kevin is carrying out a joint PhD program at DiverSE &amp\; Oran
 ge Labs.</div><div>His topics of interest include&nbsp\;Identity Management 
 in the Web context.</div><div><br></div><div>Enters <strong>Kevin</strong>:&
 nbsp\;</div><div><div style="background-color: #ffffff\;" data-mce-style="ba
 ckground-color: #ffffff\;">&nbsp\;"I will present the recent progress on my 
 thesis work (including two articles this year!) and the work to come.&nbsp\;
 </div><div style="background-color: #ffffff\;" data-mce-style="background-co
 lor: #ffffff\;">&nbsp\; &nbsp\;I will recall the basis of WebRTC\, and in pa
 rticular WebRTC security architecture. The objective is to raise the securit
 y level of a WebRTC communication on demand.</div><div style="background-col
 or: #ffffff\;" data-mce-style="background-color: #ffffff\;">To do that\, I f
 ocused on the explicit authentication system and explored two directions: to
  allow users to choose which IdP to use and to negotiate over the other part
 y authentication. Implementation details included. \;)"</div><div style="bac
 kground-color: #ffffff\;" data-mce-style="background-color: #ffffff\;"><br><
