BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:24e8ec74-6261-4fd9-9852-31baa18a4673
SUMMARY:[DiverSE] DiverSE Coffee[Jean-Emile]
DESCRIPTION:Hi All\, \n\nThe second DiverSE Coffee of the week will be prese
 nted by Jean-Emile today. He will talk about the work he will present during
  the IEEE MASCOTS (22nd October\, B-COM Rennes) on Tracking Application Fing
 erprint in a Trustless Cloud Environment for Sabotage Detection. \n\n\n\nSum
 mary: Companies are more and more inclined to use collaborative cloud resour
 ces when their maximum internal capacities are reached in order to minimize 
 their TCO. The downside of using such a collaborative cloud\, made of privat
 e clouds' unused resources\, is that malicious resource providers may sabota
 ge the correct execution of third-party-owned applications due to its uncont
 rolled nature. In this paper\, we propose an approach that allows sabotage d
 etection in a trustless environment. To do so\, we designed a mechanism that
  (1) builds an application fingerprint considering a large set of resources 
 usage (such as CPU\, I/O\, memory) in a trusted environment using random for
 est algorithm\, and (2) an online remote fingerprint recognizer that monitor
 s application execution and that makes it possible to detect unexpected appl
 ication behavior. \nOur approach has been tested by building the fingerprint
  of 5 applications on trusted machines. When running these applications on u
 ntrusted machines (with either homogeneous\, heterogeneous or unspecified ha
 rdware from the one that was used to build the model)\, the fingerprint reco
 gnizer was able to ascertain whether the execution of the application is cor
 rect or not with a median accuracy of about 98% for heterogeneous hardware a
 nd about 40% for the unspecified one. \n\n\nThe talk will take place in Turi
 ng-Petri at 1pm. \n\n\n\nBest\, \n\nJuliana. \n
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<html><body id='htmlmode'><h3>The following is 
 a new meeting request:</h3>\n\n<p>\n<table border='0'>\n<tr><th align=left>S
 ubject:</th><td>[DiverSE] DiverSE Coffee[Jean-Emile] </td></tr>\n<tr><th ali
 gn=left>Organizer:</th><td>"Juliana Alves pereira" &lt\;juliana.alves-pereir
 a@irisa.fr&gt\; </td></tr>\n</table>\n<p>\n<table border='0'>\n<tr><th align
 =left>Time:</th><td>Friday\, October 18\, 2019\, 1:00:00 PM - 2:00:00 PM GMT
  +01:00 Brussels\, Copenhagen\, Madrid\, Paris\n </td></tr></table>\n<p>\n<t
 able border='0'>\n<tr><th align=left>Invitees:</th><td>diverse@inria.fr\; ne
 lly.bencomo@inria.fr </td></tr>\n</table>\n<div>*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*</div><br
 ><div style="font-family: arial\, helvetica\, sans-serif\; font-size: 11pt\;
  color: #000000"><div><div style="font-size: 16px\; background-color: #fdfdf
 d\;" data-mce-style="font-size: 16px\; background-color: #fdfdfd\;">Hi All\,
 </div><div style="font-size: 16px\; background-color: #fdfdfd\;" data-mce-st
 yle="font-size: 16px\; background-color: #fdfdfd\;"><br data-mce-bogus="1"><
 /div><div style="font-size: 16px\; background-color: #fdfdfd\;" data-mce-sty
 le="font-size: 16px\; background-color: #fdfdfd\;">The second DiverSE Coffee
  of the week will be presented by Jean-Emile&nbsp\;today. He will talk about
  the work he will present during the IEEE MASCOTS (22nd October\, B-COM Renn
 es) on&nbsp\;<strong>Tracking Application Fingerprint in a Trustless Cloud E
 nvironment for Sabotage Detection.<br></strong><p style="margin: 0px\;" data
 -mce-style="margin: 0px\;"><strong></strong><br></p><p style="margin: 0px\;"
  data-mce-style="margin: 0px\;">Summary: Companies are more and more incline
 d to use collaborative cloud resources when their maximum internal capacitie
 s are reached in order to minimize their TCO. The downside of using such a c
 ollaborative cloud\, made of private clouds' unused resources\, is that mali
 cious resource providers may sabotage the correct execution of third-party-o
 wned applications due to its uncontrolled nature. In this paper\, we propose
  an approach that allows sabotage detection in a trustless environment. To d
 o so\, we designed a mechanism that (1) builds an application fingerprint co
 nsidering a large set of resources usage (such as CPU\, I/O\, memory) in a t
 rusted environment using random forest algorithm\, and (2) an online remote 
 fingerprint recognizer that monitors application execution and that makes it
  possible to detect unexpected application behavior.<br>Our approach has bee
 n tested by building the fingerprint of 5 applications on trusted machines. 
 When running these applications on untrusted machines (with either homogeneo
 us\, heterogeneous or unspecified hardware from the one that was used to bui
 ld the model)\, the fingerprint recognizer was able to ascertain whether the
  execution of the application is correct or not with a median accuracy of ab
 out 98% for heterogeneous hardware and about 40% for the unspecified one.</p
 ><p style="margin: 0px\;" data-mce-style="margin: 0px\;"><br></p><p style="m
 argin: 0px\;" data-mce-style="margin: 0px\;">The talk will take place in Tur
 ing-Petri at 1pm.<br></p><p style="margin: 0px\;" data-mce-style="margin: 0p
 x\;"><br></p><p style="margin: 0px\;" data-mce-style="margin: 0px\;">Best\,<
 br></p><p style="margin: 0px\;" data-mce-style="margin: 0px\;"><span id="DWT
 301" class="ZmSearchResult" style="background-color: #fffec4\;" data-mce-sty
 le="background-color: #fffec4\;">Juliana.</span></p></div></div></div></body
 ></html>
