BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:39e94c67-bd96-42c1-9c07-8ee424c3c60f
SUMMARY:DiverSE Coffee [Gunter Mussbacher]
DESCRIPTION:Hey\,\n\nWe will be pleased to have Pr. Gunter Mussbacher as a s
 peaker for the first of this week's DiverSE Coffee. He will give us a presen
 tation on his research work and in particular on the topic of "Goal Modeling
  for Sustainability and Human Values".\n\nBelow are some more information ab
 out his presentation and a short bio.\n\nThe meeting will take place in room
  Aurigny at 1pm on Thursday.\n\nBest regards\,\nJune\n\n\nAbstract:\nHuman V
 alues and Sustainability have emerged as concerns of central relevance. Whil
 e high-profile value breaches have led to financial or reputational losses f
 or companies like Delta Air Lines\, Facebook\, and Volkswagen\, human values
  - such as transparency\, fairness\, and responsibility - are heavily underr
 epresented in software engineering methods. Sustainability\, as a wicked pro
 blem\, poses challenges to business-as-usual in many areas\, including that 
 of software and systems modeling. In this talk\, I briefly present key chall
 enges for human values and sustainability before introducing goal modeling a
 s a means to capture and reason about the intentions of stakeholders who car
 e about human values and sustainability. I then report on recent efforts to 
 (i) improve the reusability of goal models so that goal models can be evalua
 ted in a reuse hierarchy with other software engineering models\, (ii) impro
 ve the evaluation of evolving goal models by explicitly defining changes to 
 a goal model over time\, and (iii) reason about the effectiveness of human d
 evelopment in complement with a technical efficiency assessment using human 
 time as the unit of measure for sustainable technological development.\n\nSh
 ort Bio:\nGunter Mussbacher is an Associate Professor in the Department of E
 lectrical and Computer Engineering at McGill University\, with 140+ publicat
 ions related to his research interests: model-driven requirements engineerin
 g\, modularity in modeling\, concern-driven development\, next-generation re
 use techniques\, sustainability\, and human values. Since 2013\, Gunter has 
 been Associate Rapporteur responsible for the User Requirements Notation (UR
 N) at Study Group 17 of the International Telecommunication Union (ITU). Gun
 ter co-edited all versions of URN\, the first goal-based and scenario-driven
  requirements modeling language standardized by ITU. He is the co-founder\, 
 organizer\, and steering committee member of the Model-Driven Requirements E
 ngineering (MoDRE) workshop series held yearly at the International Requirem
 ents Engineering Conference (RE) since 2011. He was PC Chair for SAM 2014\, 
 Finance Chair for RE 2015\, Conference Chair for ICSE 2019\, workshop/tutori
 al/publicity chair for various RE and MODELS conferences\, and regularly ser
 ves as a PC member for key conferences in his research area (e.g.\, RE\, MOD
 ELS\, SLE\, SAM).\n\n\n\n
LOCATION:"Lieu : Rennes\, Bâtiment : D\, Étage : orange\, Salle : D165" <ren
 nes-salle_aurigny@zimbra-local.inria.fr>
