BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:46855394-8d33-4e1c-8833-611caafb0528
SUMMARY:Diverse Coffee: Gerson Sunyé 
DESCRIPTION:Hi all\, \n\nAs part of our long-standing tradition\, the DiverS
 E Coffee\, we are happy to have Gerson Sunyé introduce the NeoEMF tool. \nHe
 'll take us on a journey through the ancient history of NeoEMF in order to s
 hare the experiences\, the success stories\, and lessons learned. \n\nCome a
 long and see for yourself! \n\nRegards\, \n\nBio: \n\nGerson Sunyé is an ass
 istant professor at the University of Nantes\, where he teaches different to
 pics related to Software Engineering. \nSince 2015\, he leads the AtlanMod r
 esearch team\, which is part of the LS2N research Laboratory. \nHis research
  topics are related to Model-Based Engineering\, Software Testing\, and Dist
 ributed Systems. \nGerson has also a long-established academic relationship 
 with DiverSE and currently co-advises Kevin and Mohamed’s thesis. \n\nTopic:
  \n\nThe growing use of Model Driven Engineering (MDE) techniques in industr
 y has emphasised scalability of existing model persistence solutions as a ma
 jor issue. Specifically\, there is a need to store\, query\, and transform v
 ery large models in an efficient way. \nSeveral persistence solutions based 
 on relational and NoSQL databases have been proposed to achieve scalability.
  However\, existing solutions often rely on a single data store\, which suit
 s a specific modelling activity\, but may not be optimised for other use cas
 es. \nIn response to this lack of optimised solutions\, the AtlanMod team de
 veloped NeoEMF\, a multi-database model persistence framework able to store 
 very large models in key-value stores\, graph databases\, and wide column da
 tabases. \nIn this presentation\, I will introduce the development of NeoEMF
 : many mistakes\, some successes\, and at the end\, a reliable and performan
 t tool. \n
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<html><body id='htmlmode'><h3>The following is 
 a new meeting request:</h3>\n\n<p>\n<table border='0'>\n<tr><th align=left>S
 ubject:</th><td>Diverse Coffee: Gerson Sunyé  </td></tr>\n<tr><th align=left
 >Organiser:</th><td>"Amine Benelallam" &lt\;amine.benelallam@inria.fr&gt\; <
 /td></tr>\n</table>\n<p>\n<table border='0'>\n<tr><th align=left>Location:</
 th><td>"Rennes-Salle Minquiers (B025)" &lt\;rennes-salle_minquiers@zimbra-lo
 cal.inria.fr&gt\; </td></tr>\n<tr><th align=left>Resources:</th><td>"Rennes-
 Salle Minquiers (B025)" &lt\;rennes-salle_minquiers@zimbra-local.inria.fr&gt
 \; (Lieu : Rennes\, Bâtiment : B\, Étage : bleu\, Salle : B025) </td></tr>\n
 <tr><th align=left>Time:</th><td>Thursday\, 13 April\, 2017\, 1:00:00 PM - 2
 :00:00 PM GMT +01:00 Brussels\, Copenhagen\, Madrid\, Paris\n </td></tr></ta
 ble>\n<p>\n<table border='0'>\n<tr><th align=left>Invitees:</th><td>diverse@
 inria.fr </td></tr>\n</table>\n<div>*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*</div><br><div style=
 "font-family: times new roman\, new york\, times\, serif\; font-size: 12pt\;
  color: #000000"><div>Hi all\,&nbsp\;</div><div><br></div><div>As part of ou
 r long-standing tradition\, the DiverSE Coffee\, we are happy to have Gerson
  Sunyé introduce the NeoEMF tool.&nbsp\;</div><div>He'll take us on a journe
 y through the ancient history of &nbsp\;NeoEMF in order to share the experie
 nces\, the success stories\, and lessons learned.</div><div><br></div><div>C
 ome along and see for yourself!&nbsp\;</div><div><br></div><div>Regards\,</d
 iv><div><br></div><div></div><div><strong>Bio:&nbsp\;</strong></div><div><p 
 style="margin: 0px\;" data-mce-style="margin: 0px\;">Gerson Sunyé is an assi
 stant professor at the University of Nantes\, where he teaches different top
 ics related to Software Engineering.</p><p style="margin: 0px\;" data-mce-st
 yle="margin: 0px\;">Since 2015\, he leads the AtlanMod research team\, which
  is part of the LS2N research Laboratory.</p><p style="margin: 0px\;" data-m
 ce-style="margin: 0px\;">His research topics are related to Model-Based Engi
 neering\, Software Testing\, and Distributed Systems.&nbsp\;</p><p style="ma
 rgin: 0px\;" data-mce-style="margin: 0px\;">Gerson has also a long-establish
 ed academic relationship with DiverSE and currently co-advises Kevin and Moh
 amed’s thesis.</p><p style="margin: 0px\;" data-mce-style="margin: 0px\;"><b
 r></p></div><div></div><div><strong>Topic:</strong>&nbsp\;</div><div><p styl
 e="margin: 0px\;" data-mce-style="margin: 0px\;">The growing use of Model Dr
 iven Engineering (MDE) techniques in industry has emphasised scalability of 
 existing model persistence solutions as a major issue. Specifically\, there 
 is a need to store\, query\, and transform very large models in an efficient
  way.</p><p style="margin: 0px\;" data-mce-style="margin: 0px\;">Several per
 sistence solutions based on relational and NoSQL databases have been propose
 d to achieve scalability. However\, existing solutions often rely on a singl
 e data store\, which suits a specific modelling activity\, but may not be op
 timised for other use cases.</p><p style="margin: 0px\;" data-mce-style="mar
 gin: 0px\;">In response to this lack of optimised solutions\, the AtlanMod t
 eam developed NeoEMF\, a multi-database model persistence framework able to 
 store very large models in key-value stores\, graph databases\, and wide col
 umn databases. <br>In this presentation\, I will introduce the development o
 f NeoEMF: many mistakes\, some successes\, and at the end\, a reliable and p
 erformant tool.</p></div></div></body></html>
