BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:4ac9361a-03e5-46fb-8019-8f8d6c49a38e
SUMMARY:DiverSE Coffee [Manuel Leduc]:  Modular Language Composition for the
  Masses - SLE'18
DESCRIPTION:Hi all\, \n\nWe're happy to have Manuel Leduc as a speaker for o
 ur next DiverSE Coffee. \n\nHe will be presenting his paper entitled Modular
  Language Composition for the Masses\, accepted at SLE'18. \n\nEnters Manuel
  : \nThe goal of modular language development is to enable the definition of
  new languages as assemblies of pre-existing ones. Recent approaches in this
  area are plentiful but usually suffer from two main problems: either they d
 o not support modular language composition both at the specification and imp
 lementation levels\, or they require advanced knowledge of specific paradigm
 s which hampers wide adoption in the industry. In this paper\, we introduce 
 a non-intrusive approach to modular development of language concerns with we
 ll-defined interfaces that can be composed modularly at the specification an
 d implementation levels. We present an implementation of our approach atop t
 he Eclipse Modeling Framework\, namely Alex—an object-oriented meta-language
  for semantics definition and language composition. We evaluate Alex in the 
 development of a new DSL for IoT systems modeling resulting from the composi
 tion of three independently defined languages (UML activity diagrams\, Lua\,
  and the CORBA Interface Description Language). We evaluate the effort requi
 red to implement and compose these languages using Alex with regards to simi
 lar approaches in the literature. \n\n\nThe presentation is held Thursday at
  1 pm in room Markov. \n\nKudos to Manuel end Benoit\, \n\nCheers\, \n\n
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<html><body id='htmlmode'><h3>The following mee
 ting has been modified:</h3>\n\n<p>\n<table border='0'>\n<tr><th align=left>
 Subject:</th><td>DiverSE Coffee [Manuel Leduc]:  Modular Language Compositio
 n for the Masses - SLE'18 </td></tr>\n<tr><th align=left>Organiser:</th><td>
 "Amine Benelallam" &lt\;amine.benelallam@inria.fr&gt\; </td></tr>\n</table>\
 n<p>\n<table border='0'>\n<tr><th align=left>Time:</th><td>Thursday\, 18 Oct
 ober\, 2018\, 13:00:00 - 14:00:00 GMT +01:00 Brussels\, Copenhagen\, Madrid\
 , Paris\n </td></tr></table>\n<p>\n<table border='0'>\n<tr><th align=left>In
 vitees:</th><td>manuel.leduc@inria.fr\; diverse@inria.fr </td></tr>\n</table
 >\n<div>*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*</div><br><div style="font-family: arial\, helvet
 ica\, sans-serif\; font-size: 12pt\; color: #000000"><div style="font-family
 : arial\, helvetica\, sans-serif\; font-size: 12pt\; color: #000000"><div st
 yle="font-family: arial\, helvetica\, sans-serif\; font-size: 12pt\; color: 
 #000000"><div style="font-family: arial\, helvetica\, sans-serif\; font-size
 : 12pt\; color: #000000"><div>Hi all\,&nbsp\;</div><div><br data-mce-bogus="
 1"></div><div>We're happy to have Manuel Leduc as a speaker for our next Div
 erSE Coffee.</div><div><br data-mce-bogus="1"></div><div>He will be presenti
 ng his paper entitled&nbsp\;&nbsp\;<strong>Modular Language Composition for 
 the Masses\,</strong> accepted at SLE'18.</div><div><br data-mce-bogus="1"><
 /div><div><div><span style="text-decoration: underline\;" data-mce-style="te
 xt-decoration: underline\;">Enters Manuel</span>:&nbsp\;</div>\n<div></div>\
 n<div><em>The goal of modular language development is to enable the definiti
 on of new languages as assemblies of pre-existing ones. Recent approaches in
  this area are plentiful but usually suffer from two main problems: either t
 hey do not support modular language composition both at the specification an
 d implementation levels\, or they require advanced knowledge of specific par
 adigms which hampers wide adoption in the industry. In this paper\, we intro
 duce a non-intrusive approach to </em>modular<em>&nbsp\;development of langu
 age concerns with well-defined interfaces that can be composed modularly at 
 the specification and implementation levels. We present an implementation of
  our approach atop the Eclipse Modeling Framework\, namely Alex—an object-or
 iented meta-language for semantics definition and language composition. We e
 valuate Alex in the development of a new DSL for IoT systems modeling result
 ing from the composition of three independently defined languages (UML activ
 ity diagrams\, Lua\, and the CORBA Interface Description Language). We evalu
 ate the effort required to implement and compose these languages using Alex 
 with regards to similar approaches in&nbsp\;the literature.</em></div>\n<div
 ></div></div><div><br data-mce-bogus="1"></div><div><br data-mce-bogus="1"><
 /div><div>The presentation is held Thursday at 1 pm in room Markov.<br><br><
 /div><div>Kudos to Manuel end Benoit\,&nbsp\;</div><div><br>Cheers\,&nbsp\;<
 /div><div><br data-mce-bogus="1"></div><div></div></div></div></div></div></
 body></html>
ATTENDEE;CN=Manuel Leduc;ROLE=REQ-PARTICIPANT;PARTSTAT=ACCEPTED:mailto:manue
 l.leduc@inria.fr
ATTENDEE;CN=diverse;ROLE=REQ-PARTICIPANT;PARTSTAT=NEEDS-ACTION;RSVP=TRUE:mai
 lto:diverse@inria.fr
ORGANIZER;CN=Amine Benelallam:mailto:amine.benelallam@inria.fr
