BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:5eeb3ab9-585c-43bf-b0ef-9200c4a3d90a
SUMMARY:DiverSe Coffee
DESCRIPTION:Hi everyone\,\n\nFor the DiverSE Coffee of this week\, we will h
 ave a presentation from Paul TEMPLE.\nPaul TEMPLE did his PhD here in the Di
 verSE Team and is now post-doc at the PReCISE \nresearch center at the Unive
 rsity of Namur.\n\nIn his talk\, Paul will present his work on the improveme
 nt of fairness in machine \nlearning based decision systems. This work has b
 een accepted at the BIAS Workshop\, \ncolocated with ECML/PKDD 2020.\n\nThis
  DiverSE Coffee will be online\, hence you can watch it from your office OR 
 join\nsome of us in the Petri-Turing room for a projection of the presentati
 on.\nThis time it will not be on Teams !!! I'll send the link by mail the d-
 day.\n\n\nAbstract : \nMachine learning is being integrated into a growing n
 umber of critical systems with \nfar-reaching impacts on society. Unexpected
  behaviour and unfair decision processes \nare coming under increasing scrut
 iny due to this widespread use and its theoretical \nconsiderations. Individ
 uals\, as well as organisations\, notice\, test\, and criticize \nunfair res
 ults to hold model designers and deployers accountable. We offer a framework
  \nthat assists these groups in mitigating unfair representations stemming f
 rom the \ntraining datasets. Our framework relies on two inter-operating adv
 ersaries to improve \nfairness. First\, a model is trained with the goal of 
 preventing the guessing of \nprotected attributes’ values while limiting uti
 lity losses. This first step optimizes \nthe model’s parameters for fairness
 . Second\, the framework leverages evasion attacks \nfrom adversarial machin
 e learning to generate new examples that will be misclassified.\nThese new e
 xamples are then used to retrain and improve the model in the first step. \n
 These two steps are iteratively applied until a significant improvement in f
 airness \nis obtained. We evaluated our framework on well-studied datasets i
 n the fairness \nliterature — including COMPAS — where it can surpass other 
 approaches concerning \ndemographic parity\, equality of opportunity and als
 o the model’s utility. We also \nillustrate our findings on the subtle diffi
 culties when mitigating unfairness and \nhighlight how our framework can ass
 ist model designers.\n\n\nGwendal JOUNEAUX
LOCATION:"Rennes-Salle Turing-Petri (G109-G107\, Vidéoprojecteur\, Salle sép
 arable en deux) [R]" <rennes-salle_turing-petri@zimbra-local.inria.fr>
ATTENDEE;CN=diverse;PARTSTAT=NEEDS-ACTION;RSVP=TRUE:mailto:diverse@inria.fr
ATTENDEE;CN=Bruno Lebon;PARTSTAT=NEEDS-ACTION;RSVP=TRUE:mailto:bruno.lebon@i
 nria.fr
