BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:7c55fd75-dc08-4b49-949c-8f86425f431d
SUMMARY:Diverse coffee: Arnaud Blouin
DESCRIPTION:Dear DiverSErs\, \n\nAfter his talk\, some time ago\, about UI c
 ode analysis for design smells detection\, I am happy to announce the return
  of Arnaud Blouin to report on his latest advances in this topic. \nParticul
 arly\, Arnaud will introduce one particular UI design smell he has found in 
 previous work: the Blob Listener. \nLater\, he will explain how to automatic
 ally refactor Blob Listener instances\, then elaborate on the difficulties f
 aced while using statistical tests during the analyses of the results (other
  than the WTF metric). \n\n\n\n\nThis work is intended to be submitted to th
 e IST Journal. The current draft can be found here . \n\n\nIf you want more\
 , come and see for yourself! \n\n\nRegards\, \n\nBio: \nArnaud Blouin is an 
 associate professor at the National Institutes of Applied Sciences (INSA Ren
 nes)\, France\, since September 2011\, doing research in the DiverSE team ca
 lled Triskell) at Inria / IRISA\, Rennes. \n\nArnaud develops a research act
 ivity in the field of software engineering with a focus on user interface (U
 I) engineering and model-driven engineering (MDE). \nHe currently works on t
 he validation and maintenance of user interfaces\, with a focus on the use o
 f software testing and code analysis techniques to produce\, as automaticall
 y as possible\, UI tests and detect bad UI coding practices. \nHis MDE-activ
 ities investigate how to assemble and customise domain-specific languages\, 
 and how to leverage model slicing techniques. \n\n\nAbstract : \nUser Interf
 aces (UIs) intensively rely on event-driven programming: widgets send UI eve
 nts\, which capture users' interactions\, to dedicated objects called contro
 llers. Controllers use several UI listeners that handle these events to prod
 uce UI commands. First\, we reveal the presence of design smells in the code
  that describes and controls UIs. Second\, we demonstrate that specific code
  analyses are necessary to analyze and refactor UI code\, because of its cou
 pling with the rest of the code. We conducted an empirical study on four lar
 ge Java Swing and SWT open-source software systems: Eclipse\, JabRef\, Argou
 ML \, and FreeCol. We study to what extent the number of UI commands that a 
 UI listener can produce has an impact on the change-and fault-proneness of t
 he UI listener code. We develop a static code analysis for detecting UI comm
 ands in the code. \n\n\nWe identify a new type of design smell\, called Blob
  listener that characterizes UI listeners that can produce more than two UI 
 commands. We propose a systematic static code analysis procedure that search
 es for Blob listener that we implement in InspectorGuidget. We conducted exp
 eriments on the four software systems for which we manually identified 53 in
 stances of Blob listener. InspectorGuidget successfully detected 52 Blob lis
 teners out of 53. The results exhibit a precision of 81.25 % and a recall of
  98.11 %. We then developed a semi-automatically and behavior-preserving ref
 actoring process to remove Blob listeners. 49.06 % of the 53 Blob listeners 
 were automatically refactored. Patches for JabRef\, and FreeCol have been ac
 cepted and merged. Discussions with developers of the four software systems 
 assess the relevance of the Blob listener. \n
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<html><body id='htmlmode'><h3>The following is 
 a new meeting request:</h3>\n\n<p>\n<table border='0'>\n<tr><th align=left>S
 ubject:</th><td>Diverse coffee: Arnaud Blouin </td></tr>\n<tr><th align=left
 >Organiser:</th><td>"Amine Benelallam" &lt\;amine.benelallam@inria.fr&gt\; <
 /td></tr>\n</table>\n<p>\n<table border='0'>\n<tr><th align=left>Location:</
 th><td>"Rennes-Salle Minquiers (B025)" &lt\;rennes-salle_minquiers@zimbra-lo
 cal.inria.fr&gt\; </td></tr>\n<tr><th align=left>Resources:</th><td>"Rennes-
 Salle Minquiers (B025)" &lt\;rennes-salle_minquiers@zimbra-local.inria.fr&gt
 \; (Lieu : Rennes\, Bâtiment : B\, Étage : bleu\, Salle : B025) </td></tr>\n
 <tr><th align=left>Time:</th><td>Thursday\, 20 April\, 2017\, 1:00:00 PM - 2
 :00:00 PM GMT +01:00 Brussels\, Copenhagen\, Madrid\, Paris\n </td></tr></ta
 ble>\n<p>\n<table border='0'>\n<tr><th align=left>Invitees:</th><td>diverse@
 inria.fr </td></tr>\n</table>\n<div>*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*</div><br><div style=
 "font-family: times new roman\, new york\, times\, serif\; font-size: 12pt\;
  color: #000000"><div>Dear DiverSErs\,&nbsp\;</div><div><br></div><div>After
  his talk\, some time ago\, about UI code analysis for design smells detecti
 on\, I am happy to announce the return of Arnaud Blouin to report on his lat
 est advances in this topic.<br></div><div><span style="font-size: 12pt\;">Pa
 rticularly\, Arnaud will introduce one particular UI design smell he has fou
 nd in previous work: the Blob Listener.</span></div><div><span style="font-s
 ize: 12pt\;">Later\, he will explain how to automatically refactor Blob List
 ener instances\, then elaborate on the difficulties faced while using statis
 tical tests during the analyses of the results &nbsp\;(other than the WTF me
 tric).</span></div><div><span style="font-size: 12pt\;"><br></span></div><di
 v><span style="font-size: 12pt\;"><img src="http://www.gridshore.nl/wp-conte
 nt/uploads/code-quality-measurement.jpg" alt="Code smells" data-mce-src="htt
 p://www.gridshore.nl/wp-content/uploads/code-quality-measurement.jpg"></span
 ></div><div></div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div></div><div><p style="ma
 rgin: 0px\;" data-mce-style="margin: 0px\;">This work is intended to be subm
 itted to the IST Journal. The current draft can be found <a data-mce-href="h
 ttps://hal.archives-ouvertes.fr/hal-01499106" href="https://hal.archives-ouv
 ertes.fr/hal-01499106">here<span data-mce-type="bookmark" id="mce_2_start" d
 ata-mce-style="overflow:hidden\;line-height:0px" style="overflow:hidden\;lin
 e-height:0px">﻿.</span></a></p><p style="margin: 0px\;" data-mce-style="marg
 in: 0px\;"><br></p><p style="margin: 0px\;" data-mce-style="margin: 0px\;">I
 f you want more\, come and see for yourself!</p><p style="margin: 0px\;" dat
 a-mce-style="margin: 0px\;"><br></p><p style="margin: 0px\;" data-mce-style=
 "margin: 0px\;">Regards\,</p></div><div><br></div><div><strong>Bio:&nbsp\;</
 strong></div><div><span style="font-size: 12pt\;">Arnaud Blouin is an associ
 ate professor at the National Institutes of Applied Sciences (INSA Rennes)\,
  France\, since September 2011\, doing research in the DiverSE&nbsp\;team </
 span><span style="font-size: 12pt\;"> called Triskell) at Inria / IRISA\, Re
 nnes.</span></div><div><p style="margin: 0px\;" data-mce-style="margin: 0px\
 ;"><span style="font-size: 12pt\;">Arnaud develops a research activity in th
 e field of software engineering with a focus on user interface (UI) engineer
 ing and model-driven engineering (MDE). </span></p><p style="margin: 0px\;" 
 data-mce-style="margin: 0px\;"><span style="font-size: 12pt\;">He currently 
 works on the validation and maintenance of user interfaces\, with a&nbsp\;</
 span><span style="font-size: 12pt\;">&nbsp\;focus on the use of software tes
 ting and code analysis techniques to produce\, as automatically as possible\
 , UI tests and detect bad UI coding practices.</span></p><p style="margin: 0
 px\;" data-mce-style="margin: 0px\;"><span style="font-size: 12pt\;">His MDE
 -activities&nbsp\;</span><span style="font-size: 12pt\;">investigate how to 
 assemble and customise domain-specific languages\, and how to leverage model
  slicing techniques.</span></p><p style="margin: 0px\;" data-mce-style="marg
 in: 0px\;"><span style="font-size: 12pt\;"><br></span></p><p style="margin: 
 0px\;" data-mce-style="margin: 0px\;"><span style="font-size: 12pt\;"><stron
 g>Abstract</strong>:</span></p><p style="margin: 0px\;" data-mce-style="marg
 in: 0px\;"><span style="font-size: 12pt\;"></span><span style="font-size: 12
 pt\;">User Interfaces (UIs) intensively rely on event-driven programming: wi
 dgets send UI events\, which capture users' interactions\, to dedicated obje
 cts called controllers.&nbsp\;</span><span style="font-size: 12pt\;">Control
 lers use several UI listeners that handle these events to produce UI command
 s. &nbsp\;&nbsp\;</span><span style="font-size: 12pt\;">First\, we reveal th
 e presence of design smells in the code that describes and controls UIs.&nbs
 p\;</span><span style="font-size: 12pt\;">Second\, we demonstrate that speci
 fic code analyses are necessary to analyze and refactor UI code\, because of
  its coupling with the rest of the code.&nbsp\;</span><span style="font-size
 : 12pt\;">We conducted an empirical study on four large Java Swing and SWT o
 pen-source software systems: Eclipse\, JabRef\, </span><span style="font-siz
 e: 12pt\;">ArgouML</span><span style="font-size: 12pt\;">\, and FreeCol. We 
 study to what extent the number of UI commands that a UI listener can produc
 e has an impact on the change-and fault-proneness of the UI listener code. W
 e develop a static code analysis for detecting UI commands in the code.</spa
 n></p><p style="margin: 0px\;" data-mce-style="margin: 0px\;"><span style="f
 ont-size: 12pt\;"><br></span></p><p style="margin: 0px\;" data-mce-style="ma
 rgin: 0px\;"><span style="font-size: 12pt\;">We identify a new type of desig
 n smell\, called Blob listener that characterizes UI listeners that can prod
 uce more than two UI commands. We propose a systematic static code analysis 
 procedure that searches for Blob listener that we implement in InspectorGuid
 get. We conducted experiments on the four software systems for which we manu
 ally identified 53 instances of Blob listener. InspectorGuidget successfully
  detected 52 Blob listeners out of 53. The results exhibit a precision of 81
 .25 % and a recall of 98.11 %. We then developed a semi-automatically and be
 havior-preserving refactoring process to remove Blob listeners. 49.06 % of t
 he 53 Blob listeners were automatically refactored. Patches for JabRef\, and
  FreeCol have been accepted and merged. Discussions with developers of the f
 our software systems assess the relevance of the Blob listener.&nbsp\;</span
 ></p><p style="margin: 0px\;" data-mce-style="margin: 0px\;"><span style="fo
 nt-size: 12pt\;"></span></p></div></div></body></html>
