BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:8d619dec-a0fe-4712-bc01-b7673825a29f
SUMMARY:Diverse Coffee
DESCRIPTION:Hi all\, \n\nThis week\, I and Hugo will be giving a test presen
 tation of our FOSD[1] talk on "Learning Software Configuration Spaces".\nIt 
 would be really nice to have your feedback.\n\nEnters @Juliana: \nTitle: Lea
 rning Software Configuration Spaces: A Systematic Literature Review\nMost mo
 dern software systems (operating systems like Linux or Android\, Web browser
 s like Firefox or Chrome\, video encoders like ffmpeg\, x264 or VLC\, mobile
  and cloud applications\, etc.) are highly-configurable. Hundreds of configu
 ration options\, features\, or plugins can be combined\, each potentially wi
 th distinct functionality and effects on execution time\, security\, energy 
 consumption\, etc. Due to the combinatorial explosion and the cost of execut
 ing software\, it is quickly impossible to exhaustively explore the whole co
 nfiguration space. Hence\, numerous works have investigated the idea of lear
 ning it from a small sample with practical interests for both developers and
  end-users of configurable systems. In this survey\, we report on the differ
 ent applicability including use-cases (performance prediction\, configuratio
 n optimization\, constraint mining\, etc.)\, targeted software systems and a
 pplication domains. We review the various strategies employed to gather a re
 presentative and cost-effective sample\; the automated software techniques u
 sed to measure functional and non-functional properties of configurations\; 
 the learning algorithms and their evaluations\n\nEnters @Hugo: \nTitle: Mach
 ine Learning for Performance Specialization of Configurable Systems\nAs the 
 growing variability of software becomes more of a problem than a solution fo
 r their users unable to handle the resulting complexity\, a need for help ha
 s emerged. At the same time\, machine learning techniques rise in popularity
  and accessibility\, offering multiple solutions to help the user\, mainly a
 bout performance prediction. We propose a different approach to the problem 
 in the form of an automated specialization of a configurable system. Based o
 n the interpretability of decisions trees\, we explored some processes leadi
 ng to a specialized system corresponding to a user-specific needs and compar
 ed them. Our results show good performance of each process\, as well as open
 ing ways to improvement.\n\nThe presentation is held on Thursday\, March 7th
  in room Markov at 1 pm.\n\n[1] The Feature Oriented Software Development (F
 OSD) meeting is a yearly informal meeting to bring together researchers work
 ing on feature-oriented software development. See more at https://digital-ba
 uhaus.github.io/website-FOSD-2019/\n\nKind regards\,\nJuliana.\n
LOCATION:"Rennes-Salle Markov (G105\, Vidéoprojecteur) [R]" <rennes-salle_ma
 rkov@zimbra-local.inria.fr>
ATTENDEE;CN=diverse;PARTSTAT=NEEDS-ACTION;RSVP=TRUE:mailto:diverse@inria.fr
X-MS-OLK-SENDER:mailto:juliana.alves-pereira@irisa.fr
ORGANIZER;CN=Fanny Banor;SENT-BY="mailto:juliana.alves-pereira@irisa.fr":mai
 lto:fanny.banor@irisa.fr
X-MS-OLK-SENDER:mailto:juliana.alves-pereira@irisa.fr
DTSTART;TZID="Europe/Brussels":20190307T130000
DTEND;TZID="Europe/Brussels":20190307T140000
