BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:9b657691-5afc-4c99-883f-2028c879a122
SUMMARY:[SEMINAR] Towards efficient support of model evolution management (T
 . Leveque)
DESCRIPTION:\nModel Driven Engineering (MDE) aims at mastering software prod
 uct complexity undertaking all software engineering activities on models. Ex
 cept some specific domains such as critical systems\, MDE is not yet widely 
 adopted by large software manufacturers. One reason is that\, in practice\, 
 in MDE software projects\, an application is a mixture of metamodels\, model
 s and code which are all developed manually. Models are expected to evolve d
 ue to refinements\, improvements\, bugfixes\, and so forth. Because of the s
 ame reasons\, also modelling languages (i.e. metamodels) and code are expect
 ed to be changed\, even though at a different speed if compared to models. C
 onsistent versionning of metamodels\, models and code is needed. This talk w
 ill present a first step solution in providing the concepts and mechanisms n
 eeded to solve these issues. The proposed solution is based on formal defini
 tion of evolution policies as annotations on meta models. A policy enforces 
 version consistency and evolution ripple effects in each workspace\, through
  a bi-directional synchronization between model elements and files. Finally\
 , A policy also defines and enforces the cooperative work strategy between c
 oncurrent workspaces. \n
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<html><body><h3>La réunion suivante a été modif
 iée :</h3>\n\n<p>\n<table border='0'>\n<tr><th align=left>Sujet :</th><td>[S
 EMINAR] Towards efficient support of model evolution management (T. Leveque)
  [MODIFIÉ]</td></tr>\n<tr><th align=left>Organisateur:</th><td>"Benoit Combe
 male" &lt\;benoit.combemale@irisa.fr&gt\; </td></tr>\n</table>\n<p>\n<table 
 border='0'>\n<tr><th align=left>Endroit :</th><td>Rennes-Salle Lipari (F202)
  </td></tr>\n<tr><th align=left>Ressources :</th><td>Rennes-Salle Lipari (F2
 02) (Lieu : Rennes\, Bâtiment : F\, Étage : orange\, Salle : F202) </td></tr
 >\n<tr><th align=left>Heure:</th><td>Lundi 19 Mars 2012\, 13:00:00 - 14:00:0
 0 GMT +01:00 Bruxelles\, Copenhague\, Madrid\, Paris\n </td></tr></table>\n<
 p>\n<table border='0'>\n<tr><th align=left>Invités:</th><td>"erwan" &lt\;erw
 an.bousse@gmail.com&gt\;\; "Antonio-Junior Mattos" &lt\;antonio-junior.matto
 s@irisa.fr&gt\;\; "An Phung-Khac" &lt\;an.phungkhac@inria.fr&gt\;\; "Julien 
 Richard-Foy" &lt\;julien.richard-foy@irisa.fr&gt\;\; "Joao Ferreira_filho" &
 lt\;joao.ferreira_filho@inria.fr&gt\;\; "Suresh Pillay" &lt\;suresh.pillay@i
 risa.fr&gt\;\; "Jean-Emile Dartois" &lt\;jean-emile.dartois@inria.fr&gt\;\; 
 "Jonathan Marchand" &lt\;jonathan.yves.marchand@irisa.fr&gt\;\; "Hanen Haoua
 s" &lt\;hanen.haouas@inria.fr&gt\;\; "Xavier Dolques" &lt\;xavier.dolques@in
 ria.fr&gt\;\;  ... </td></tr>\n</table>\n<div>*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*</div><br><
 div style='font-family:times new roman\,new york\,times\,serif\; font-size: 
 12pt\; color: #000000\;'><div style="font-family:times new roman\,new york\,
 times\,serif\; font-size: 12pt\; color: #000000\;">Model Driven Engineering 
 (MDE) aims at mastering software product complexity undertaking all software
  engineering activities on models. Except some specific domains such as crit
 ical systems\, MDE is not yet widely adopted by large software manufacturers
 . One reason is that\, in practice\, in MDE software projects\, an applicati
 on is a mixture of metamodels\, models and code which are all developed manu
 ally. Models are expected to evolve due to refinements\, improvements\, bugf
 ixes\, and so forth. Because of the same reasons\, also modelling languages 
 (i.e. metamodels) and code are expected to be changed\, even though at a dif
 ferent speed if compared to models.&nbsp\; Consistent versionning of metamod
 els\, models and code is needed. This talk will present a first step solutio
 n in providing the concepts and mechanisms needed to solve these issues. The
  proposed solution is based&nbsp\; on formal definition of evolution policie
 s as annotations on meta models. A policy enforces version consistency and e
 volution ripple effects in each workspace\, through a bi-directional synchro
 nization between model elements and files. Finally\, A policy also defines a
 nd enforces the cooperative work strategy between concurrent workspaces.<br>
 </div></div></body></html>
LOCATION:Rennes-Salle Lipari (F202)
