BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:9e698fc4-89b0-4a02-b28c-86f012eadc7a
SUMMARY:Diverse e-Coffee
DESCRIPTION:\nHey everyone\, \nI apologise for the late announcement\, I've 
 been pretty busy the last few days and I skipped organising the coffee. I'm 
 sorry. \n\nI will still make a presentation on the paper recently submitted 
 for publication to ICCS (International Conference on Computational Science).
  The abstract is below. \n\nHere are the information for the Zoom meeting: \
 n[ https://zoom.us/j/97023606074?pwd=Q2Z0d3VNOXppVkd4K1lCR0R5eG5tQT09 | http
 s://zoom.us/j/97023606074?pwd=Q2Z0d3VNOXppVkd4K1lCR0R5eG5tQT09 ] \n\nMeeting
  ID : 970 2360 6074 \nPassword : 088408 \n\nAs usual\, the presentation will
  start at 1:30pm but you can join from 1pm to discuss about the team news an
 d so on. :) \nLooking forward to hearing your feedback. \n\nSee you later\, 
 \nJune \n\n\nAbstract: \n"Trading off some accuracy for better performances 
 in scientific computing is an appealing approach to ease the exploration of 
 \nvarious alternatives on complex simulation models. Existing approaches inv
 olve the application of either time-consuming model reduction techniques or 
 resource-demanding statistical approaches. Such requirements prevent any opp
 ortunistic model exploration\, e.g.\, exploring various scenarios on environ
 mental models. This limits the ability to analyse new models for scientists\
 , to support trade-off analysis for decision-makers and to empower the gener
 al public towards informed environmental intelligence. In this paper\, we pr
 esent a new approximate computing technique\, aka. loop aggregation\, which 
 consists in automatically reducing the main loop of a simulation model by ag
 gregating the corresponding spatial or temporal data. We apply this approxim
 ate scientific computing approach on a geophysical model of a hydraulic simu
 lation with various input data. The experimentation demonstrates the ability
  to drastically decrease the simulation time while preserving acceptable res
 ults with a minimal set-up. We obtain a median speed-up of 95.13% and up to 
 99.78% across all the 23 case studies." \n\n
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<html><body id='htmlmode'><h3>Nouvelle demande 
 de réunion ci-dessous :</h3>\n\n<p>\n<table border='0'>\n<tr><th align=left>
 Sujet :</th><td>Diverse e-Coffee </td></tr>\n<tr><th align=left>Organisateur
 :</th><td>"June Benvegnu-Sallou" &lt\;june.benvegnu-sallou@irisa.fr&gt\; </t
 d></tr>\n</table>\n<p>\n<table border='0'>\n<tr><th align=left>Heure:</th><t
 d>Jeudi 16 Avril 2020\, 13:15:00 - 14:30:00 GMT +01:00 Bruxelles\, Copenhagu
 e\, Madrid\, Paris\n </td></tr></table>\n<p>\n<table border='0'>\n<tr><th al
 ign=left>Invités:</th><td>diverse@inria.fr </td></tr>\n</table>\n<div>*~*~*~
 *~*~*~*~*~*~*</div><br><div style="font-family: arial\, helvetica\, sans-ser
 if\; font-size: 12pt\; color: #000000"><div><p>Hey everyone\,</p>\n<p>I apol
 ogise for the late announcement\, I've been pretty busy the last few days an
 d I skipped organising the coffee. I'm sorry.<br>\n  <br>\nI will still make
  a presentation on the paper recently submitted for \npublication to ICCS (I
 nternational Conference on Computational Science).\n The abstract is below.<
 br>\n</p>\n<p>Here are the information for the Zoom meeting:</p>\n<p><a href
 ="https://zoom.us/j/97023606074?pwd=Q2Z0d3VNOXppVkd4K1lCR0R5eG5tQT09">https:
 //zoom.us/j/97023606074?pwd=Q2Z0d3VNOXppVkd4K1lCR0R5eG5tQT09</a><br>\n  <br>
 \nMeeting ID : 970 2360 6074<br>\nPassword : 088408<br>\n</p>\n<p>As usual\,
  the presentation will start at 1:30pm but you can join from 1pm to discuss 
 about the team news and so on. :)</p>\n<p>Looking forward to hearing your fe
 edback.<br>\n</p>\n<p>See you later\,</p>\n<p>June</p>\n<p><br>\n</p>\n<p>Ab
 stract:</p>\n<p>"Trading off some accuracy for better performances in scient
 ific computing is an appealing approach to ease the exploration of<br>\nvari
 ous alternatives on complex simulation models. Existing approaches \ninvolve
  the application of either time-consuming model reduction \ntechniques or re
 source-demanding statistical approaches. Such \nrequirements prevent any opp
 ortunistic model exploration\, e.g.\, \nexploring various scenarios on envir
 onmental models. This limits the \nability to analyse new models for scienti
 sts\, to support trade-off \nanalysis for decision-makers and to empower the
  general public towards \ninformed environmental intelligence. In this paper
 \, we present a new \napproximate computing technique\, aka. loop aggregatio
 n\, which consists \nin automatically reducing the main loop of a simulation
  model by \naggregating the corresponding spatial or temporal data. We apply
  this \napproximate scientific computing approach on a geophysical model of 
 a hydraulic simulation with various \ninput data. The experimentation demons
 trates the ability to drastically \ndecrease the simulation time while prese
 rving acceptable results with a \nminimal set-up. We obtain a median speed-u
 p of 95.13% and up to 99.78% \nacross all the 23 case studies."<br>\n  <br>\
 n</p>\n</div></div></body></html>
