BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:d75a4b5c-c349-4c4f-8265-af66ddfb0402
SUMMARY:DiverSE Coffee [Stefanos Skalistis]: MDE & Time-critical systems: Br
 inging the gap of theory and practice
DESCRIPTION:The following meeting has been modified:\n\nSubject: DiverSE Cof
 fee [Stefanos Skalistis]:  [MODIFIED]\nOrganiser: amine.benelallam@inria.fr 
 [MODIFIED]\n\nLocation: "Rennes-Salle Lipari (F202 Vidéoprojecteur\, Visioco
 nférence)" <rennes-salle_lipari@zimbra-local.inria.fr>\nTime: Thursday\, 28 
 February 2019 from 13:00 - 14:00 Europe/Brussels\n\nInvitees: "Stefanos Skal
 istis" <stefanos.skalistis@inria.fr>\; "Amine Benelallam" <amine.benelallam@
 inria.fr> [MODIFIED]\n\n*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*\n\nHi all\, \n\nWe're happy to h
 ave Stefanos Skalistis as our next DiverSE Coffee speaker. Stefanos is curre
 ntly a Postdoctoral Researcher at PACAP team\, IRISA/INRIA\, working on Time
 -Critical Systems and WCET estimation for multi-core architectures. He recei
 ved his M.Sc. (2011) from Aristotle University of Thessaloniki\n(AUTh\, Gree
 ce) and his Ph.D. (2017) from the Swiss Federal Polytechnic School of Lausan
 ne (EPFL\,Switzerland) under the supervision of Prof. Joseph Sifakis and Pro
 f. Giovanni DeMicheli. Prior to his Ph.D. studies he has worked in research 
 positions for 2.5 years\, conducting research in Component-based systems\, S
 oftware Reuse\, Model Transformations and Autonomous vehicles. His current r
 esearch interests include Real-time systems\, Correctness-by-construction te
 chniques\, Scheduling and Optimisation.\n\nEnters Stefanos: \nWhile multi-ma
 ny-core architectures has enabled scaling of the majority of computing syste
 ms\, the same can not be argued for time-critical systems. In time-critical 
 systems\, the timing behaviour must be modelled in order to provide timing-g
 uarantees for the tasks of the executed applications. A significant factor t
 hat inhibits time-critical systems from scaling is timing interference\, i.e
 . delays caused by simultaneous access to hardware-arbitrated shared resourc
 es\, such as busses\, on-chip memories\, NoC\, etc.\, which alters the timin
 g behaviour non-deterministically. The models found in the literature for ti
 ming behaviours are either state-based models (e.g timed-automata) or task-b
 ased models of computation (e.g. task graphs\, data-flow graphs)\, each one 
 having its merits depending on the desired property to be validated. For exa
 mple\, state-based models are typically used to validate deadlock freedom\, 
 whereas task-based models are typically used to validate schedulability and 
 freedom of timing-anomalies. In this presentation\, I will present recent wo
 rks regarding interference-sensitive methods and how Model-Driven Engineerin
 g can assist the development\, efficiency and adaptability of time-critical 
 systems.\n\nThe presentation is held in room Lipari\, tomorrow at 1 pm.\n\nK
 ind regards\,
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<html><body><h3>The following meeting has been 
 modified:</h3>\n\n<p>\n<table border='0'>\n<tr><th valign='top' align='left'
 >Subject:</th><td>DiverSE Coffee [Stefanos Skalistis]: MDE &amp\; Time-criti
 cal systems: Bringing the gap of theory and practice [MODIFIED]</td></tr>\n<
 tr><th valign='top' align='left'>Organiser:</th><td>amine.benelallam@inria.f
 r</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n<p>\n<table border='0'>\n<tr><th valign='top' align
 ='left'>Location:</th><td>"Rennes-Salle Lipari (F202 Vidéoprojecteur\, Visio
 conférence)" &lt\;rennes-salle_lipari@zimbra-local.inria.fr&gt\;</td></tr>\n
 <tr><th valign='top' align='left'>Time:</th><td>Thursday\, 28 February 2019 
 from 13:00 - 14:00 <span style='color: #686357'>Europe/Brussels</span></td><
 /tr>\n</table>\n<p>\n<table border='0'>\n\n<tr><th valign='top' align='left'
 >Invitees:</th><td>"Stefanos Skalistis" &lt\;stefanos.skalistis@inria.fr&gt\
 ;\; "Amine Benelallam" &lt\;amine.benelallam@inria.fr&gt\;\; "diverse" &lt\;
 diverse@inria.fr&gt\; [MODIFIED]</td></tr></table>\n<div>*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*
 </div><br>The following meeting has been modified:<br /><br />Subject: Diver
 SE Coffee [Stefanos Skalistis]: &nbsp\;[MODIFIED]<br />Organiser: amine.bene
 lallam@inria.fr [MODIFIED]<br /><br />Location: "Rennes-Salle Lipari (F202 V
 idéoprojecteur\, Visioconférence)" &lt\;rennes-salle_lipari@zimbra-local.inr
 ia.fr&gt\;<br />Time: Thursday\, 28 February 2019 from 13:00 - 14:00 Europe/
 Brussels<br /><br />Invitees: "Stefanos Skalistis" &lt\;stefanos.skalistis@i
 nria.fr&gt\;\; "Amine Benelallam" &lt\;amine.benelallam@inria.fr&gt\; [MODIF
 IED]<br /><br />*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*<br /><br />Hi all\, <br /><br />We're ha
 ppy to have Stefanos Skalistis as our next DiverSE Coffee speaker. Stefanos 
 is currently a Postdoctoral Researcher at PACAP team\, IRISA/INRIA\, working
  on Time-Critical Systems and WCET estimation for multi-core architectures. 
 He received his M.Sc. (2011) from Aristotle University of Thessaloniki<br />
 (AUTh\, Greece) and his Ph.D. (2017) from the Swiss Federal Polytechnic Scho
 ol of Lausanne (EPFL\,Switzerland) under the supervision of Prof. Joseph Sif
 akis and Prof. Giovanni DeMicheli. Prior to his Ph.D. studies he has worked 
 in research positions for 2.5 years\, conducting research in Component-based
  systems\, Software Reuse\, Model Transformations and Autonomous vehicles. H
 is current research interests include Real-time systems\, Correctness-by-con
 struction techniques\, Scheduling and Optimisation.<br /><br />Enters Stefan
 os: <br />While multi-many-core architectures has enabled scaling of the maj
 ority of computing systems\, the same can not be argued for time-critical sy
 stems. In time-critical systems\, the timing behaviour must be modelled in o
 rder to provide timing-guarantees for the tasks of the executed applications
 . A significant factor that inhibits time-critical systems from scaling is t
 iming interference\, i.e. delays caused by simultaneous access to hardware-a
 rbitrated shared resources\, such as busses\, on-chip memories\, NoC\, etc.\
 , which alters the timing behaviour non-deterministically. The models found 
 in the literature for timing behaviours are either state-based models (e.g t
 imed-automata) or task-based models of computation (e.g. task graphs\, data-
 flow graphs)\, each one having its merits depending on the desired property 
 to be validated. For example\, state-based models are typically used to vali
 date deadlock freedom\, whereas task-based models are typically used to vali
 date schedulability and freedom of timing-anomalies. In this presentation\, 
 I will present recent works regarding interference-sensitive methods and how
  Model-Driven Engineering can assist the development\, efficiency and adapta
 bility of time-critical systems.<br /><br />The presentation is held in room
  Lipari\, tomorrow at 1 pm.<br /><br />Kind regards\, </body></html>
LOCATION:"Rennes-Salle Lipari (F202 Vidéoprojecteur\, Visioconférence)" <ren
 nes-salle_lipari@zimbra-local.inria.fr>
