BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:e8d2c164-7a3d-4e8e-b3c1-ebb0b1da6aa1
SUMMARY:[SEMINAR] Formal approaches to non-functional properties of componen
 t-based and service-oriented applications (S. Zschaler)
DESCRIPTION:Component-based software engineering and service-oriented archit
 ecture are two important and closely related techniques for developing compl
 ex software systems. While composing functionality from smaller parts (compo
 nents or services) is well understood\, the composition of non-functional pr
 operties such as performance\, security\, reliability\, or accuracy is still
  an area of intensive research. In this talk\, we start by presenting a form
 al framework of concepts that will enable us to study such compositions more
  closely. We then continue to highlight some interesting research challenges
  that can be tackled atop this framework. Some of these will have substantia
 l influence on the work to be done at King's College London as part of the r
 ecently launched RELATE International Training Network on Engineering and Pr
 ovisioning of Service Based Cloud Applications. King's College London is cur
 rently recruiting students interested in pursuing a PhD. Consequently\, the 
 talk will also give some insights into RELATE\, eligibility criteria\, and t
 he application procedure. \n\nThe RELATE network funds PhD positions startin
 g next september. These funds are granted by the Marie Curie program\, one o
 f the most prestigious fellowship in European academia. Relate PhD positions
  are of particularly high value\, with an emphasis on mobility: PhD candidat
 es will visit several teams extensively during the PhD work\, and they will 
 have very good opportunities to work jointly with teams well known at the in
 ternational level.
LOCATION:turing
ATTENDEE;ROLE=REQ-PARTICIPANT;PARTSTAT=NEEDS-ACTION;RSVP=TRUE:mailto:triskel
 l@irisa.fr
