BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:f829807d-fefd-4fd6-b396-e675561d395a
SUMMARY:DiverSE eCoffee : [Mathieu Acher] Programming the Chess Puzzle Space
DESCRIPTION:\nHey everyone! \nFor tomorrow's DiverSE eCoffee\, Mathieu will 
 talk about a fun project dealing with chess\, (artificial) intelligence\, pr
 ogramming\, DSL and notebooks! So everything to pick the interest of every m
 ember of the team! You can read the abstract below to see for yourself \;). 
 \nAs usual\, the DiverSE eCoffee starts at 1:30 pm but we can join from 1:15
  to discuss informally and share some news. \n\nThe information to join the 
 Zoom meeting (Thanks Benoit): \n[ https://us02web.zoom.us/j/88163421264?pwd=
 WUlXc1RvL0lwQ2E0SUJRTEFrQXZHQT09 | https://us02web.zoom.us/j/88163421264?pwd
 =WUlXc1RvL0lwQ2E0SUJRTEFrQXZHQT09 ] \n\nMeeting ID : 881 6342 1264 \nPasswor
 d : 063005 \nSee you soon\, \nJune \n------ \n\nAbstract : \n"I want to tell
  a story about computational thinking\, (artificial) intelligence\, and the 
 joy of programming. \nEverything started with a chess puzzle shared by a fri
 end on social media and instead of silently ignoring\, I launched a Jupyter 
 notebook. \nI will explain this notebook. I will first explain how I naïvely
  iterated until finding a program that fits in a Tweet thanks to opportunist
 ic reuse and some Python hacks. \nI will show how I made vary the 280 charac
 ters and build a kind of DSL to resolve other chess puzzles that pop out. \n
 Then I realized: instead of finding a solution to a puzzle\, why not generat
 ing puzzles? \nThe rough idea is simple: once you know how to resolve a puzz
 le\, you can try to synthesize puzzles that have solution(s). \nThe difficul
 t challenge is to synthesize puzzles that are "hard" and "interesting" enoug
 h. \nI will briefly illustrate some attempts to explore the "chess puzzle sp
 ace" with grand-masters' opinions in the loop. \nFinally\, I will discuss th
 e applicability of these ideas to any kind of puzzles (with some examples be
 yond chess). \nStated differently\, I'm defending a new life style: whenever
  you see a puzzle\, launch your notebook\, generalize\, generate\, and move 
 to another puzzle space \;=)" \n
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<html><body id='htmlmode'><h3>La réunion suivan
 te a été modifiée :</h3>\n\n<p>\n<table border='0'>\n<tr><th align=left>Suje
 t :</th><td>DiverSE eCoffee : [Mathieu Acher] Programming the Chess Puzzle S
 pace </td></tr>\n<tr><th align=left>Organisateur:</th><td>"June Benvegnu-Sal
 lou" &lt\;june.benvegnu-sallou@irisa.fr&gt\; </td></tr>\n</table>\n<p>\n<tab
 le border='0'>\n<tr><th align=left>Heure:</th><td>Jeudi 30 Avril 2020\, 13:1
 5:00 - 14:30:00 GMT +01:00 Bruxelles\, Copenhague\, Madrid\, Paris [MODIFIÉ]
 \n </td></tr></table>\n<p>\n<table border='0'>\n<tr><th align=left>Invités:<
 /th><td>diverse@inria.fr\; antoine.cheron@fabernovel.com\; oscar.vera-perez@
 inria.fr\; luc.lesoil@irisa.fr\; emmanuel.chebbi@inria.fr\; quentin.le-dilav
 rec@irisa.fr\; pierre.jeanjean@inria.fr\; gwendal.jouneaux@irisa.fr </td></t
 r>\n</table>\n<div>*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*</div><br><div style="font-family: ari
 al\, helvetica\, sans-serif\; font-size: 12pt\; color: #000000"><div style="
 font-family: arial\, helvetica\, sans-serif\; font-size: 12pt\; color: #0000
 00"><div><p>Hey everyone!</p>\n<p>For tomorrow's DiverSE eCoffee\, Mathieu w
 ill talk about a fun project dealing with chess\, (artificial) intelligence\
 , programming\, DSL and \nnotebooks! So everything to pick the interest of e
 very member of the \nteam! You can read the abstract below to see for yourse
 lf \;).</p>\n<p>As usual\, the DiverSE eCoffee starts at 1:30 pm but we can 
 join from 1:15 to discuss informally and share some news.<br>\n</p>\n<p>The 
 information to join the Zoom meeting (Thanks Benoit):</p>\n<p><a href="https
 ://us02web.zoom.us/j/88163421264?pwd=WUlXc1RvL0lwQ2E0SUJRTEFrQXZHQT09" rel="
 noopener" target="_blank">https://us02web.zoom.us/j/88163421264?pwd=WUlXc1Rv
 L0lwQ2E0SUJRTEFrQXZHQT09</a><br data-mce-bogus="1"></p>\n<p>Meeting ID : 881
  6342 1264<br>Password : 063005</p>\n<p>See you soon\,</p>\n<p>June</p>\n<p>
 ------ <br>\n</p>\n<p>Abstract :</p>\n<p>"I want to tell a story about compu
 tational thinking\, (artificial) intelligence\, and the joy of programming. 
 <br>\nEverything started with a chess puzzle shared by a friend on social \n
 media and instead of silently ignoring\, I launched a Jupyter notebook. <br>
 \nI will explain this notebook. I will first explain how I naïvely \niterate
 d until finding a program that fits in a Tweet thanks to \nopportunistic reu
 se and some Python hacks. <br>\nI will show how I made vary the 280 characte
 rs and build a kind of DSL to resolve other chess puzzles that pop out. <br>
 \nThen I realized: instead of finding a solution to a puzzle\, why not gener
 ating puzzles? <br>\nThe rough idea is simple: once you know how to resolve 
 a puzzle\, you can try to synthesize puzzles that have solution(s). <br>\nTh
 e difficult challenge is to synthesize puzzles that are "hard" and "interest
 ing" enough. <br>\nI will briefly illustrate some attempts to explore the "c
 hess puzzle space" with grand-masters' opinions in the loop. <br>\nFinally\,
  I will discuss the applicability of these ideas to any kind of puzzles (wit
 h some examples beyond chess). <br>\nStated differently\, I'm defending a ne
 w life style: whenever you see a \npuzzle\, launch your notebook\, generaliz
 e\, generate\, and move to another \npuzzle space \;=)"<br>\n</p>\n</div></d
 iv></div></body></html>
ATTENDEE;CN=diverse;ROLE=REQ-PARTICIPANT;PARTSTAT=NEEDS-ACTION;RSVP=TRUE:mai
 lto:diverse@inria.fr
