BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:fe6c4897-84d0-4119-a030-e41319c0e4dd
SUMMARY:[SEMINAR] Why Engineering Pervasive Applications is so complex? (J. 
 Bourcier)
DESCRIPTION:\nPervasive computing systems typically consist of multiple devi
 ces and software entities capable of interacting with one another. Various t
 ypes of software-equipped devices may be available for various purposes\, su
 ch as interacting with the real environment\, providing display and control 
 services to users\, or exposing data and application interfaces to other dev
 ices. In this context\, the main challenge is thus to provide coherent perva
 sive environments\, offering interesting high-level applications and service
 s\, based on an entanglement of heterogeneous\, distributed and dynamic devi
 ces and software services\, communicating via various technologies and proto
 cols. \nPervasive environment are mainly characterized by their openness and
  constant evolution. These two particular characteristics bring very challen
 ging issues when building a pervasive application such as: Distribution\, He
 terogeneity\, Plural Authority Cohabitation\, Security\, Privacy\, Safety\, 
 Scalability\, Dynamism\, Context awareness\, Mobility and so on. Thus\, by i
 ts inherent nature a pervasive application is a self-adaptive application. \
 nIn this talk\, I will present in more details all these challenges and expl
 ain to what extent this bring new challenges in the software engineering com
 munity. I will then present H-Omega\, a service-oriented middleware dedicate
 d to the execution of smart home applications\, which deals with part of the
  presented challenges (+ Demonstration). I will conclude this talk by presen
 ting the research tracks I am willing to explore and an open discussion. 
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<html><body><h3>La réunion suivante a été modif
 iée :</h3>\n\n<p>\n<table border='0'>\n<tr><th align=left>Sujet :</th><td>[S
 EMINAR] Why Engineering Pervasive Applications is so complex? (J. Bourcier) 
 </td></tr>\n<tr><th align=left>Organisateur:</th><td>"Benoit Combemale" &lt\
 ;benoit.combemale@irisa.fr&gt\; </td></tr>\n</table>\n<p>\n<table border='0'
 >\n<tr><th align=left>Endroit :</th><td>lipari </td></tr>\n<tr><th align=lef
 t>Heure:</th><td>Mardi 1 Février 2011\, 12:30:00 - 13:30:00 GMT +01:00 Bruxe
 lles\, Copenhague\, Madrid\, Paris\n </td></tr></table>\n<p>\n<table border=
 '0'>\n<tr><th align=left>Invités:</th><td>"Hang Nguyen" <hang.nguyen@inria.f
 r>\; "Xavier Dolques" <xavier.dolques@inria.fr>\; "Aymeric Hervieu" <aymeric
 .hervieu@irisa.fr>\; "Clement Guy" <clement.guy@inria.fr>\; "Naouel Moha" <n
 aouel.moha@irisa.fr>\; "Loic Lesage" <loic.lesage@inria.fr>\; "Olivier-Natha
 nael Ben_david" <olivier-nathanael.ben_david@inria.fr>\; "Tam Le_nhan" <tam.
 le_nhan@inria.fr>\; "Nicolas Sannier" <nicolas.sannier@irisa.fr>\; "Mahmoud 
 Ben_hassine" <mahmoud.ben_hassine@inria.fr>\;  ... </td></tr>\n</table>\n<di
 v>*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*</div><br><div style='font-family:Times New Roman\; fon
 t-size: 12pt\; color: #000000\;'><div style="font-family:Times New Roman\; f
 ont-size: 12pt\; color: #000000\;">Pervasive computing systems typically con
 sist of multiple devices and software entities capable of interacting with o
 ne another. Various types of software-equipped devices may be available for 
 various purposes\, such as interacting with the real environment\, providing
  display and control services to users\, or exposing data and application in
 terfaces to other devices. In this context\, the main challenge is thus to p
 rovide coherent pervasive environments\, offering interesting high-level&nbs
 p\; applications and services\, based on an entanglement of heterogeneous\, 
 distributed and dynamic devices and software services\, communicating via va
 rious technologies and protocols.<br>Pervasive environment are mainly charac
 terized by their openness and constant evolution. These two particular chara
 cteristics bring very challenging issues when building a pervasive applicati
 on such as:&nbsp\; Distribution\, Heterogeneity\, Plural Authority Cohabitat
 ion\, Security\, Privacy\, Safety\, Scalability\, Dynamism\, Context awarene
 ss\, Mobility and so on. Thus\, by its inherent nature a pervasive applicati
 on is a self-adaptive application.<br>In this talk\, I will present in more 
 details all these challenges and explain to what extent this bring new chall
 enges in the software engineering community. I will then present H-Omega\, a
  service-oriented middleware dedicated to the execution of smart home applic
 ations\, which deals with part of the presented challenges (+ Demonstration)
 . I will conclude this talk by presenting the research tracks I am willing t
 o explore and an open discussion.</div></div></body></html>
