BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:fe8d4c3d-1259-4b93-9f93-36847a705c31
SUMMARY:DiverSE Coffee [Alejandro Gomez boix]
DESCRIPTION:Hi All\, \n\nWe'll have Alejandro on our next DiverSE Coffee pre
 senting his paper: "A Collaborative Strategy for Mitigating Tracking through
  Browser Fingerprinting". \nPaper accepted in the 6th ACM Workshop on Moving
  Target Defense (MTD 2019) in conjunction with ACM CCS 2019. \n\nEnters @Ale
 jandro: Browser fingerprinting is a technique that collects information abou
 t the browser configuration and the environment in which it is running. \nTh
 is information is so diverse that it can partially or totally identify users
  online. \nOver time\, several countermeasures have emerged to mitigate trac
 king through browser fingerprinting. \nHowever\, these measures do not offer
  full coverage in terms of privacy protection\, as some of them may introduc
 e inconsistencies or unusual behaviors\, making these users stand out from t
 he rest. \nWe address these limitations by proposing a novel approach that m
 inimizes both the identifiability of users and the required changes to brows
 er configuration. \nTo this end\, we exploit clustering algorithms to identi
 fy the devices that are prone to share the same or similar fingerprints and 
 to provide them with a new non-unique fingerprint. \nWe then use this finger
 print to automatically assemble and run web browsers through virtualization 
 within a docker container. \nThus all the devices in the same cluster will e
 nd up running a web browser with an indistinguishable and consistent fingerp
 rint. \nAh Maybe I will bring some candies\, maybe carambars\, I do not know
 \, or cookies\, no better no\, forget it. \n\nThe presentation is held in ro
 om Aurigny 31th October at 1 pm. \n\nKind regards\, \nJuliana. \n
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<html><body id='htmlmode'><h3>The following mee
 ting has been modified:</h3>\n\n<p>\n<table border='0'>\n<tr><th align=left>
 Subject:</th><td>DiverSE Coffee [Alejandro Gomez boix] </td></tr>\n<tr><th a
 lign=left>Organizer:</th><td>"Juliana Alves pereira" &lt\;juliana.alves-pere
 ira@irisa.fr&gt\; </td></tr>\n</table>\n<p>\n<table border='0'>\n<tr><th ali
 gn=left>Location:</th><td>"Rennes-Salle Aurigny (D165\, Vidéoprojecteur\, Vi
 sioconférence)" &lt\;rennes-salle_aurigny@zimbra-local.inria.fr&gt\; </td></
 tr>\n<tr><th align=left>Resources:</th><td>"Rennes-Salle Aurigny (D165\, Vid
 éoprojecteur\, Visioconférence)" &lt\;rennes-salle_aurigny@zimbra-local.inri
 a.fr&gt\; (Rennes-Salle Aurigny (D165\, Vidéoprojecteur\, Visioconférence)) 
 </td></tr>\n<tr><th align=left>Time:</th><td>Thursday\, November 7\, 2019\, 
 1:00:00 PM - 2:00:00 PM GMT +01:00 Brussels\, Copenhagen\, Madrid\, Paris [M
 ODIFIED]\n </td></tr></table>\n<p>\n<table border='0'>\n<tr><th align=left>I
 nvitees:</th><td>diverse@inria.fr\; wide@inria.fr\; alejandro.gomez-boix@inr
 ia.fr\; nelly.bencomo@inria.fr\; manuel.leduc@irisa.fr\; djamel-eddine.khell
 adi@irisa.fr\; pierre.jeanjean@inria.fr\; caroline.landry@inria.fr </td></tr
 >\n</table>\n<div>*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*</div><br><div style="font-family: aria
 l\, helvetica\, sans-serif\; font-size: 11pt\; color: #000000"><div style="f
 ont-family: arial\, helvetica\, sans-serif\; font-size: 11pt\; color: #00000
 0"><div><div>Hi All\,&nbsp\;</div><div><br></div><div>We'll have Alejandro o
 n our next DiverSE Coffee presenting his paper: "A Collaborative Strategy fo
 r Mitigating Tracking through Browser Fingerprinting".&nbsp\;</div><div>Pape
 r accepted in the 6th ACM Workshop on Moving Target Defense (MTD 2019) in co
 njunction with ACM CCS 2019.</div><div><br></div><div>Enters @Alejandro: Bro
 wser fingerprinting is a technique that collects information about the brows
 er configuration and the environment in which it is running.</div><div>This 
 information is so diverse that it can partially or totally identify users on
 line.</div><div>Over time\, several countermeasures have emerged to mitigate
  tracking through browser fingerprinting.</div><div>However\, these measures
  do not offer full coverage in terms of privacy protection\, as some of them
  may introduce inconsistencies or unusual behaviors\, making these users sta
 nd out from the rest.</div><div>We address these limitations by proposing a 
 novel approach that minimizes both the identifiability of users and the requ
 ired changes to browser configuration.</div><div>To this end\, we exploit cl
 ustering algorithms to identify the devices that are prone to share the same
  or similar fingerprints and to provide them with a new non-unique fingerpri
 nt.</div><div>We then use this fingerprint to automatically assemble and run
  web browsers through virtualization within a docker container.</div><div>Th
 us all the devices in the same cluster will end up running a web browser wit
 h an indistinguishable and consistent fingerprint.</div><div>Ah Maybe I will
  bring some candies\, maybe carambars\, I do not know\, or cookies\, no bett
 er no\, forget it.</div><div><br></div><div>The presentation&nbsp\;is held i
 n room Aurigny 31th October at 1 pm.&nbsp\;</div><div><br></div><div>Kind re
 gards\,</div><div>Juliana.</div></div></div></div></body></html>
LOCATION:"Rennes-Salle Aurigny (D165\, Vidéoprojecteur\, Visioconférence)" <
 rennes-salle_aurigny@zimbra-local.inria.fr>
